1
00:00:19,005 --> 00:00:33,155
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you ever think about what I was saying yesterday, the... what's the... what's the point of the practice?

2
00:00:34,737 --> 00:00:36,638
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the measurable point?

3
00:00:38,539 --> 00:00:40,561
[SPEAKER_04]: What does the successful practice result in?

4
00:00:41,922 --> 00:00:45,464
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, obviously, there are many ways you can answer this, but let's just start answering it.

5
00:01:01,117 --> 00:01:06,441
[SPEAKER_04]: One way would be not being bothered as much.

6
00:01:09,583 --> 00:01:23,712
[SPEAKER_04]: And how that... how is that practically measured, in terms of insight, that the suttas will talk about?

7
00:01:26,066 --> 00:01:27,886
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you know that you're along the right lines?

8
00:01:28,206 --> 00:01:49,151
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, yeah, because what can happen is, you know, people can own a kind of practice, because they got inspired to practice, but they will kind of inadvertently still be addressing the circumstances, avoiding and so on, and in the end, like, build up a little environment that nothing bothers them.

9
00:01:49,987 --> 00:01:57,460
[SPEAKER_04]: And then everything is sort of at the arm's length, so then they feel like, oh, look, my practice is succeeding, because I don't suffer as much, factually.

10
00:01:58,736 --> 00:02:03,560
[SPEAKER_02]: But over time, you do it, you know, one, two, three years, you start being able to manage things.

11
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:04,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, exactly.

12
00:02:04,241 --> 00:02:04,681
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

13
00:02:04,701 --> 00:02:08,104
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, you become a bit more hardened, a hardened individual.

14
00:02:08,685 --> 00:02:09,485
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

15
00:02:09,966 --> 00:02:17,332
[SPEAKER_04]: But so you wouldn't, you wouldn't say that, you wouldn't, you wouldn't, you couldn't use that as a, as I said, as a measure of successful practice.

16
00:02:17,412 --> 00:02:20,555
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, because what insight do you have?

17
00:02:21,016 --> 00:02:25,940
[SPEAKER_00]: What about if your mind moves on account of the thought of you dying?

18
00:02:27,035 --> 00:02:27,295
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah?

19
00:02:27,535 --> 00:02:27,816
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

20
00:02:27,956 --> 00:02:36,982
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how then... what insight is required for the mind to not move on account of the thought of dying?

21
00:02:37,842 --> 00:02:40,024
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the culminating insight there?

22
00:02:42,726 --> 00:02:44,847
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what is often talked about.

23
00:02:45,528 --> 00:02:45,928
[SPEAKER_03]: Anicca.

24
00:02:47,309 --> 00:02:48,870
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, what's the insight of that anicca?

25
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:51,672
[SPEAKER_04]: What the anicca and dukkha culminate in?

26
00:02:52,252 --> 00:02:53,513
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's the not-self.

27
00:02:53,713 --> 00:02:54,153
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

28
00:02:54,454 --> 00:02:55,655
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that is the measure.

29
00:02:56,671 --> 00:03:03,460
[SPEAKER_04]: The complete cessation, well, first of the self-view, then your notions of personality and conceit.

30
00:03:04,802 --> 00:03:11,451
[SPEAKER_04]: And when that is absent, completely gone, without any traces, then, yeah, you couldn't suffer even if you want to.

31
00:03:12,851 --> 00:03:19,013
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it is the measure that the less somebody takes things personally, the more they are progressing in the practice?

32
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:19,453
[SPEAKER_04]: Could be.

33
00:03:20,053 --> 00:03:25,214
[SPEAKER_04]: But, you know, you have, like, psychotherapeutical methods that teach you how to not take things personally.

34
00:03:25,294 --> 00:03:26,674
[SPEAKER_04]: But you're taking that personally.

35
00:03:27,955 --> 00:03:30,795
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there has to be an actual insight into anatta.

36
00:03:31,696 --> 00:03:32,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's, like, not...

37
00:03:32,856 --> 00:03:36,997
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not something you can stumble upon accidentally, that anatta.

38
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:44,117
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where you need the learning, you need the yonisu, manasikāra, you need practice, sense of strength.

39
00:03:44,257 --> 00:03:48,779
[SPEAKER_04]: But yeah, it's a factual, irreversible insight, to put it that way.

40
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:57,443
[SPEAKER_04]: And yeah, the whole anicca, sīla, anicca, dukkha, it's just so that you can actually experience the anatta.

41
00:03:58,303 --> 00:04:01,525
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, what is anatta?

42
00:04:08,502 --> 00:04:15,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I... because it... that is really what makes the difference.

43
00:04:16,026 --> 00:04:37,998
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's not like... yeah, because I've talked about this before, how you can say, okay, my mind... your mind won't be bothered by, let's say, even the thought of dying, but it's more fundamental than just, sort of,

44
00:04:42,552 --> 00:04:44,453
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, nothing bothers you.

45
00:04:44,473 --> 00:04:45,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That's necessary.

46
00:04:45,494 --> 00:04:48,256
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a prerequisite for the inside of...

47
00:04:48,436 --> 00:04:56,321
[SPEAKER_04]: But, as the Buddha said himself, in other, you know, dispensations at the time, there were teachers that you could factually establish neither perception nor perception.

48
00:04:56,341 --> 00:04:58,723
[SPEAKER_04]: That means you're pretty much unbothered by anything.

49
00:04:58,743 --> 00:04:58,963
[SPEAKER_04]: Anything.

50
00:04:59,343 --> 00:05:01,544
[SPEAKER_04]: Anything inferior to that, as the suttas would say.

51
00:05:02,725 --> 00:05:11,148
[SPEAKER_04]: But he said, only in his teaching, only in this presentation of the Buddhas, you will get the anatta part inside.

52
00:05:14,169 --> 00:05:21,612
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, yeah, when I'm being bothered, and even the suttas say, you know, Arahant's mind does not get overwhelmed by things, Arahant's mind overwhelms things and so on.

53
00:05:21,632 --> 00:05:28,435
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's a factual strength that is developed, that obviously without which you couldn't understand and

54
00:05:29,433 --> 00:05:33,495
[SPEAKER_04]: You fully developed the anatta, but there is that part of anatta.

55
00:05:33,675 --> 00:05:33,895
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

56
00:05:34,736 --> 00:05:41,119
[SPEAKER_04]: So, say, you have been sense restraints in the day and exercise for guarding the senses, keeping the precepts, learning in solitude.

57
00:05:44,221 --> 00:05:52,885
[SPEAKER_04]: So, okay, you're meeting the requirements, let's say like that, to start seeing atta slash anatta, attavada, abandoning it.

58
00:05:54,246 --> 00:05:56,287
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is that?

59
00:05:57,632 --> 00:06:09,180
[SPEAKER_04]: This is like something that would just occur and then you could interpret it in hindsight or do you need to know where to look for in order to see it and then start looking up until you recognize it.

60
00:06:15,284 --> 00:06:15,884
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, let's start.

61
00:06:15,944 --> 00:06:17,705
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the necessary basis for an atta?

62
00:06:22,028 --> 00:06:22,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing atta?

63
00:06:23,129 --> 00:06:23,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

64
00:06:23,409 --> 00:06:23,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Peripheral?

65
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:24,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

66
00:06:25,114 --> 00:06:26,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is atta then?

67
00:06:26,215 --> 00:06:27,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's define that first.

68
00:06:27,975 --> 00:06:29,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always peripheral.

69
00:06:29,196 --> 00:06:30,877
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't put it in front of you.

70
00:06:31,397 --> 00:06:37,180
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the properties of atta, the properties of the sense of self, would be...

71
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:38,400
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not directly visible.

72
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:41,482
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you can't actually see with your senses.

73
00:06:42,550 --> 00:06:49,292
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't observe it with your five senses and you certainly can't just... oh, yeah, this is myself that I'm just relating to.

74
00:06:49,312 --> 00:06:51,213
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's always ambiguous.

75
00:06:51,853 --> 00:06:52,073
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

76
00:06:52,474 --> 00:06:52,854
[SPEAKER_04]: What else?

77
00:06:54,214 --> 00:06:55,315
[SPEAKER_04]: Atta, the sense of self.

78
00:06:56,055 --> 00:06:57,956
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the center of experience.

79
00:06:57,976 --> 00:06:59,576
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but let's not speak theoretically.

80
00:07:00,076 --> 00:07:06,779
[SPEAKER_04]: Let's instead... like, everybody now has a sense of self to some degree, I presume.

81
00:07:09,722 --> 00:07:10,162
[SPEAKER_04]: Describe.

82
00:07:10,242 --> 00:07:10,582
[SPEAKER_04]: What is it?

83
00:07:10,943 --> 00:07:13,644
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say that it's automatic.

84
00:07:13,724 --> 00:07:30,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless one is actively seeking to clarify that ambiguous sense of self, it is, yeah, it is automatically.

85
00:07:30,771 --> 00:07:31,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatic.

86
00:07:31,791 --> 00:07:33,412
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense that whenever you experience

87
00:07:35,012 --> 00:07:37,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

88
00:07:37,635 --> 00:07:43,481
[SPEAKER_04]: But, we're talking about the sense of self that lingers always in the background of it.

89
00:07:44,983 --> 00:07:46,265
[SPEAKER_04]: So, regardless of your reactions.

90
00:07:47,166 --> 00:07:50,269
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you have the experience of when that self's not there?

91
00:07:50,890 --> 00:07:51,090
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

92
00:07:52,773 --> 00:08:03,798
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's always, to some degree, you know, sometimes might feel clearer, sometimes might not, but that sense of I am, sense of self, sense of the, yeah, center of the experience, it's always there.

93
00:08:04,758 --> 00:08:06,859
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but do you see it right now, as we speak?

94
00:08:07,059 --> 00:08:12,621
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you see your own sense of self, enduring there, on its own, somehow, somewhere, ambiguously, just kind of lingering?

95
00:08:14,522 --> 00:08:15,002
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you have it?

96
00:08:15,102 --> 00:08:16,143
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you have that sense of self?

97
00:08:21,425 --> 00:08:22,005
[SPEAKER_03]: To some degree.

98
00:08:23,793 --> 00:08:25,734
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's the key there, basically.

99
00:08:26,714 --> 00:08:39,238
[SPEAKER_04]: The way to start seeing it is to learn how to see it on its own degree, not how to make it into an object that I think it should be of my observation, of my direct attention.

100
00:08:40,619 --> 00:08:44,600
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the, again, the solitude and the practice of mindfulness, as we said before.

101
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:47,741
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, as we said yesterday, in particular, you know,

102
00:08:48,869 --> 00:08:51,450
[SPEAKER_04]: Mindfulness is memory of your present experience.

103
00:08:51,610 --> 00:08:52,430
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what sati is.

104
00:08:53,290 --> 00:08:56,431
[SPEAKER_04]: There's nothing complicated about it.

105
00:08:56,471 --> 00:09:02,333
[SPEAKER_04]: If you were to abandon the views of mindfulness being the concentrating on the present moment, I'm mindful and I'm here, I'm here.

106
00:09:02,373 --> 00:09:06,555
[SPEAKER_04]: Having the memory of the present moment right here while we speak, that's where the mind... that's where mindfulness is.

107
00:09:07,375 --> 00:09:08,695
[SPEAKER_04]: Practice of memory here and now.

108
00:09:09,235 --> 00:09:16,998
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like a a different way, a different angle, let's say like that, of attending to

109
00:09:18,330 --> 00:09:20,551
[SPEAKER_04]: What is here and now in front of me directly attended.

110
00:09:21,992 --> 00:09:28,215
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not something you can... like if I ask you recall a memory or something, just remember something, where would you... how would you do that?

111
00:09:28,795 --> 00:09:32,917
[SPEAKER_04]: Would you stare at things and feel the sensations of the body and focus to get memory?

112
00:09:32,957 --> 00:09:33,137
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

113
00:09:33,157 --> 00:09:36,079
[SPEAKER_04]: You would quite opposite, unfocus from what you were focused on.

114
00:09:36,979 --> 00:09:44,223
[SPEAKER_04]: To recall, recollect what is there, that point of view of memory,

115
00:09:45,502 --> 00:09:50,224
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether it's memory of this second while I'm saying memory, memory, memory, it's there.

116
00:09:51,004 --> 00:09:52,545
[SPEAKER_04]: Or it's the memory from ten years ago.

117
00:09:53,805 --> 00:09:54,285
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter.

118
00:09:54,485 --> 00:09:55,446
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the same point of view.

119
00:09:55,866 --> 00:10:06,030
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the point of view that you can use to observe the present experience at the same time you are in the present experience, just indirectly.

120
00:10:06,990 --> 00:10:11,812
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's the background of your current absorbed attention point of view, of your senses.

121
00:10:12,612 --> 00:10:13,133
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the contact.

122
00:10:13,153 --> 00:10:13,953
[SPEAKER_04]: It's where the mind is.

123
00:10:16,756 --> 00:10:25,698
[SPEAKER_04]: And that ambiguous sense of self is kind of here and there, neither here nor there, in between the two.

124
00:10:25,798 --> 00:10:38,361
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're of the view that that sense of self is something you can think directly, something you can access your senses directly, something you can attend, observe and see it as anatta, you're not seeing the sense of self.

125
00:10:38,921 --> 00:10:41,882
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just chasing the idea that you have intellectually developed.

126
00:10:43,092 --> 00:10:46,375
[SPEAKER_04]: of it, on account of, you know, reading the suttas and so on, listening to the talks.

127
00:10:47,095 --> 00:10:53,200
[SPEAKER_04]: The point is, Tantra Self is always behind from where you're looking.

128
00:10:55,581 --> 00:11:03,888
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, when you realize that the range, for lack of a better word,

129
00:11:04,486 --> 00:11:14,336
[SPEAKER_04]: of where you can look is either directly attended or the direction of that memory point of view, sati, you realize, oh, you don't need to know specifically where the sense of self is.

130
00:11:14,456 --> 00:11:17,038
[SPEAKER_04]: You know that it will have to be between the two points of view.

131
00:11:17,879 --> 00:11:19,100
[SPEAKER_04]: They're there at the same time.

132
00:11:20,561 --> 00:11:22,083
[SPEAKER_04]: As we said yesterday, with this, this is.

133
00:11:23,958 --> 00:11:30,180
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not that the memory point of view, where the mind is, comes first, in a sense, it exists on its own, and then the senses come up.

134
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:30,380
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

135
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,540
[SPEAKER_04]: They exist simultaneously.

136
00:11:32,221 --> 00:11:35,362
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, one is the way of the direct attention, that you can access the world.

137
00:11:35,382 --> 00:11:38,603
[SPEAKER_04]: The other one is the knowledge of you with the direct attention, that you can access the world.

138
00:11:39,383 --> 00:11:42,084
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why sati memory is used interchangeably in the suttas.

139
00:11:42,584 --> 00:11:46,325
[SPEAKER_04]: The problem is that people, when they hear the memory, they have a view of past, implicit past.

140
00:11:46,425 --> 00:11:49,486
[SPEAKER_04]: But, even if it's the past, it's presently recollected.

141
00:11:50,775 --> 00:11:59,658
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you realize, okay, so, I could technically, as a practice, instead of only recollecting the past, I could recollect what is presently enduring.

142
00:11:59,978 --> 00:12:04,199
[SPEAKER_04]: Just not allow myself to directly attend it, but know it on the level of memory.

143
00:12:05,079 --> 00:12:06,299
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, are you seated now?

144
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:07,480
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

145
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:08,700
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's memory, see?

146
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:13,301
[SPEAKER_04]: The immediate answer, you don't need to, wait, I need to feel my knees.

147
00:12:14,222 --> 00:12:19,083
[SPEAKER_04]: The recognition of that simultaneously enduring point of view is there.

148
00:12:22,443 --> 00:12:26,244
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like two points of observance of the same thing.

149
00:12:27,084 --> 00:12:32,705
[SPEAKER_04]: And because there are two, neither can be one in charge, master, experience.

150
00:12:34,845 --> 00:12:35,786
[SPEAKER_04]: So, with this, this is.

151
00:12:35,986 --> 00:12:37,046
[SPEAKER_04]: Without this, this wouldn't be.

152
00:12:37,946 --> 00:12:39,366
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, there's no room for ownership.

153
00:12:40,006 --> 00:12:45,447
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, that's all good and well in theory and that aspect, but...

154
00:12:45,467 --> 00:12:48,328
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, practically, let's say you start practicing sati correctly,

155
00:12:49,054 --> 00:12:53,556
[SPEAKER_04]: In the solitude, you're recollecting what you're presently doing, instead of attending to what you're presently doing.

156
00:12:54,556 --> 00:13:00,778
[SPEAKER_04]: So, keeping that peripheral point of view, watching what is presently, you know, sitting, breathing, whatever.

157
00:13:00,838 --> 00:13:02,419
[SPEAKER_04]: Memory of breathing, ānāpānasati.

158
00:13:02,619 --> 00:13:03,439
[SPEAKER_04]: That's exactly what it is.

159
00:13:03,479 --> 00:13:04,719
[SPEAKER_04]: Memory of current breathing.

160
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:11,942
[SPEAKER_04]: But not accessing it to the point of view of directly attending the sensations of it, but accessing it to the indirect point of view of knowledge of it.

161
00:13:12,102 --> 00:13:15,963
[SPEAKER_04]: The recognition of the of the images of breathing that you're doing, basically.

162
00:13:16,063 --> 00:13:16,523
[SPEAKER_04]: The memory.

163
00:13:17,204 --> 00:13:17,984
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what memory is.

164
00:13:18,785 --> 00:13:30,896
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the sense of self, the atta, however ambiguous it might be, however fleeting or unclear it might be, it is real as such, as I keep saying.

165
00:13:33,178 --> 00:13:43,127
[SPEAKER_04]: So, instead of you trying to see it on the level of directly attended experience, or myself, myself, you need to see it for whatever it is, however it is, a vague cloud,

166
00:13:44,519 --> 00:13:46,220
[SPEAKER_04]: Sometimes more palpable, sometimes not.

167
00:13:46,300 --> 00:13:49,382
[SPEAKER_04]: But the sense of self, sense of I am, is there lingering.

168
00:13:51,604 --> 00:13:53,705
[SPEAKER_04]: And you can't go behind it, in other words.

169
00:13:53,905 --> 00:13:56,467
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't become the observer of it and put it as an object.

170
00:13:56,707 --> 00:13:58,028
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always going to be behind you.

171
00:13:58,709 --> 00:14:05,013
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how do you make it then not perpetually escape from you?

172
00:14:06,614 --> 00:14:09,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think this elusivity

173
00:14:10,125 --> 00:14:12,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's important in terms of seeing it in its own terms.

174
00:14:12,446 --> 00:14:13,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah.

175
00:14:13,326 --> 00:14:15,147
[SPEAKER_04]: So, first you realize, okay, so I cannot not...

176
00:14:15,427 --> 00:14:16,707
[SPEAKER_04]: I cannot make it not elusive.

177
00:14:16,767 --> 00:14:17,587
[SPEAKER_04]: It's gonna stay elusive.

178
00:14:17,808 --> 00:14:21,769
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I need to accept ambiguity of it as its characteristic.

179
00:14:22,729 --> 00:14:27,591
[SPEAKER_04]: So, ambiguity should be enough for me to recognize it as being there.

180
00:14:28,531 --> 00:14:32,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Because everybody recognizes it ambiguously, but then it has the pressure, the impulse, the tendency to...

181
00:14:32,912 --> 00:14:34,333
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, let me clarify it.

182
00:14:35,167 --> 00:14:37,728
[SPEAKER_04]: The only way I can relate to it is if it is more defined.

183
00:14:38,008 --> 00:14:38,468
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's not.

184
00:14:38,508 --> 00:14:41,190
[SPEAKER_04]: It's an ambiguous phenomenon.

185
00:14:41,850 --> 00:14:44,351
[SPEAKER_04]: It's neither here nor there, but it is real as such.

